# AYXA Planner Pro — Educational Showcase Studio

Project name: **AYXA Planner Pro — Educational Showcase**

This directory is the film studio for the AYXA Planner Pro application.

Two things live here:

1. **The client walkthrough tour — the current deliverable.** A silent, driven screen
   recording of the whole app, so the client can see the product and the changes without
   reading a pitch. One stop per module, module label drawn locally in the edit.
2. **The educational showcase assets — built, not shipped.** 24 scene documents, 27 captured
   stills, shot cards and passports. The stills are still useful as documentation, and the
   narration stage exists, but the narrated film is not the deliverable.

Neither is an advertisement. Every feature shown is a real screen captured from the running app.

## Folder map

| Path | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `assets/characters/` | Reserved. This film has no human characters; the persistence surfaces are registered as props. |
| `assets/locations/` | One captured screen per module. Each is a location with its own passport. |
| `assets/props/` | Graphic devices: `@phone_frame`, `@card_paper`, `@brand_mark`, `@shell_bottom_nav`, `@stylus`. |
| `prompts/` | One shot-card file per scene, and one versioned generation prompt per shot. |
| `generations/` | Raw unapproved attempts, one folder per scene. |
| `selects/` | Approved takes only, one folder per scene. |
| `edit/` | Timeline assembly. `edit/tour/` holds the recordings, `edit/fonts/` the `drawtext` fonts, `edit/qc/` the QC frames. |
| `color/` | Grade, contrast and RTL/typography passes. |
| `sound/` | Narration, ambience bed and mix. Not used by the client tour, which is silent by design. |
| `master/` | Final deliverables. |
| `docs/` | Breakdown, bible, registry, generation log, text tasks. |

## The three studio laws

1. **Only `/selects/` is visible to the edit.**
2. **Nobody but the prompt engineer enters `/generations/`.**
3. **Reference files are never renamed; a new version is a new file.**

`/generations/` holds raw attempts. `/selects/` holds approved takes.
There are no exceptions to these three laws.

## Why the laws matter here

A generated or captured frame is cheap and disposable; an *approved* frame is a contract.
When the edit can only see `/selects/`, a bad take can never leak into the master by accident.
Because the screens in this film are captured from the live application, law 3 is what keeps
every caption reproducible: if a module changes, the old capture keeps its name and the new
capture gets a new one, so the visual bible always describes a file that still exists.

## Pipeline

`setup → studio-init → film-breakdown → reference-board → asset-passport → stress-test → shot-prompt`

- `setup` — connect the tools and share the configuration across agents.
- `studio-init` — scaffold this tree, the templates and the laws.
- `film-breakdown` — turn the module inventory into scenes and shot cards.
- `reference-board` — define approved references, captions and bans.
- `asset-passport` — write verbatim descriptors and reference-sheet prompts.
- `stress-test` — test each draft asset and lock only the repeatable ones.
- `shot-prompt` — turn a shot card plus locked passports into the fixed prompt.
